# Session wrap-up

End-of-session housekeeping. Use this when stepping away from a session that's reached a natural pause — work shipped, work blocked, or just done for the day.

## Steps

### 1. Audit dangling commits

```
git status
git log @{u}..HEAD --oneline
```

If commits are local-only, decide:
- **Push them** if they're complete work
- **Reset / discard** if they're a half-thought-through detour
- **Stash** if they're work-in-progress you'll resume next session

Don't leave commits stranded; they invisibly accumulate and confuse future-you.

### 2. Audit pushed-but-no-PR work

```
git log origin/{{MAIN_BRANCH}}..HEAD --oneline
```

If the branch is ahead of `{{MAIN_BRANCH}}` but no PR exists, you have unmerged work. Decide:
- **Ship it** — run the project's `verify` skill first, then `ship-pr` (or `ship-direct`)
- **Continue next session** — note in the task tracker that work is paused

### 3. Audit dead code and data

A session leaves residue. Code paths get superseded but not deleted; experiments leave output directories; fixtures and scratch files outlive the work that needed them. Dead code and dead data both rot — they confuse future readers, inflate the repo, and make "is this load-bearing?" un-answerable at a glance. Wrap-up is the checkpoint where you clear it.

**Dead code** — scan for what this session orphaned:

- Functions, modules, or branches no longer referenced by anything (the session replaced the caller, or the approach changed mid-flight)
- Files left behind by a discarded approach — a half-built module, a superseded helper
- Commented-out blocks and `// removed` / `// old` markers — git history already carries the old version

**Dead data** — scan for artifacts that outlived their purpose:

- Output / result directories from runs that failed, were superseded, or were one-off experiments
- Stale fixtures, scratch files, large logs, `*.tmp`, `*-old.*`, `.bak` files
- Generated artifacts checked in by mistake, or kept long past their use

For each item, decide **remove** or **archive**:

- **Remove** when it's truly dead and recoverable from git history if ever needed again. Default to remove — git is the archive for code.
- **Archive** when it has standalone reference value that git history doesn't surface well: a result set worth comparing against later, a fixture documenting a tricky case, an experiment whose *output* (not its code) is the artifact. Move it to the project's archive location (`doc/archive/`, `archive/`, or wherever the project keeps these) rather than deleting — and note in one line *why* it was kept.

Commit the cleanup on its own:

```
git add -A && git commit -m "chore: remove dead code and data from <session topic>"
```

Keep this commit separate from feature work so the diff is reviewable as pure deletion. Note what was removed or archived in the session checkpoint (step 5) so future-you knows the residue was handled deliberately, not lost.

Skip this step only when the session genuinely produced no residue — a one-behavior bug fix that touched two lines. When in doubt, scan; the audit is a 2-3 minute pass.

### 4. Reconcile task state

{{IF TASK_SOURCE_GITHUB}}
For every issue you've been working on:

```
gh issue view <id>
```

Reconcile the status:
- PR open: status should be `{{LABEL_IN_REVIEW}}`
- Still working: status should be `{{LABEL_IN_PROGRESS}}`
- PR merged: issue auto-closes; project board moves to Done automatically
- Stepping away mid-work: leave a comment summarizing where you left off and any open questions

```
gh issue edit <id> --add-label <new-status> --remove-label <old-status>
gh issue comment <id> --body "Pausing here. Next: ..."
```
{{ENDIF}}{{IF TASK_SOURCE_TASKS}}
For every row in `{{TASKS_FILE}}` you own:

- PR open: `{{RECORDS_HELPER}} tasks set <id> --status {{STATUS_IN_REVIEW}}`
- Still working: leave Status at `{{STATUS_IN_PROGRESS}}` (no helper call needed)
- PR merged: `{{RECORDS_HELPER}} tasks set <id> --status {{STATUS_DONE}}`

**Then write the session's work into each owned task's doc** — this is the step that stops reasoning dying in the transcript:

```
{{RECORDS_HELPER}} tasks note <id> --text "<what was tried, what it cost, what was ruled out and why>"
```

Where you left off goes here too, not in the row. The Notes cell is a one-line hook and a pointer; `{{RECORDS_HELPER}} lint` fails it if prose grows back into it.

```
git add {{TASKS_FILE}}
git commit -m "chore(tasks): reconcile status"
```
{{ENDIF}}

### 5. Append the session entry ({{SESSIONS_FILE}})

**Required every session, including ones that produced nothing.**

Write it with the helper rather than by hand — it prepends newest-first and renders the commit and branch links against `{{FORGE_URL}}` for you:

```
{{RECORDS_HELPER}} add session --session-id <id> --text "…" --commits <sha>,<sha> --branch <name> --next "…"
```

The entry it produces, and the shape to match if you ever do write one by hand:

```
## YYYY-MM-DD · <session-id>
<Two to four sentences: what was attempted, what landed, what was abandoned and why.
State outcomes, not activity.>
Commits: [`<sha>`]({{FORGE_URL}}/commit/<sha>), ...
Branch: [`<name>`]({{FORGE_URL}}/src/branch/<name>)
Findings: <n> added, <n> withdrawn
Next: <the concrete next step, or "-">
```

**The session id is the filename stem of this session's transcript**, which lives at `~/.claude/projects/<project-slug>/<session-id>.jsonl`:

```
ls -t ~/.claude/projects/*/*.jsonl | head -1     # most recent transcript
```

Use the **full identifier** (`c37b572c-2dfe-4b11-84ec-841ec1b9b64b`), not a truncation. A short form reads more nicely and is useless for the one job the field has: pasting it somewhere to find the transcript again. Without it the entry cannot be traced back to what actually happened.

Link every commit; a bare SHA is not clickable and will not be looked up.

`Next:` is the handoff — the field most often needed and least often written. Fill it even when the answer is "nothing, this line is closed."

This file is **never read at startup** and grows without limit by design. That is what makes it safe to be complete here.

### 6. Update the findings index ({{FINDINGS_FILE}})

Step 5 records *what happened*. This records *what the project now knows* — and unlike step 5 it is loaded into **every future session**, so every line costs context forever. Treat the budget as real.

Scan the session for anything clearing a **high bar**: a result that contradicted an assumption, an approach ruled out and why, a constraint hit the hard way, a number that will be quoted later. Skip routine progress and anything obvious from the diff.

Three distinct actions, and the last two matter as much as the first:

1. **Add** a finding with `{{RECORDS_HELPER}} add finding --status ✓ --commit <sha> --text "…" [--doc <path>]`, not a hand-edit — it renders the entry in the file's own format and inserts it newest-first. One finding per entry, with a linked commit and a `→ doc/…` pointer for anything needing more than two sentences. No commit means no entry.
2. **Withdraw** a finding this session disproved — flip it to `✗`, strike the claim, leave it in place. A wrong entry left visible is the cheapest protection against repeating the wrong turn. Never delete.
3. **Run `{{RECORDS_HELPER}} lint` and leave it green.** It checks what re-reading cannot: that every commit link resolves to a real commit, that no `→` pointer has gone dead, that provenance markers are valid, and that a claims row cites what was searched.{{IF FINDINGS_CAP}} It also enforces the **{{FINDINGS_CAP}}-line cap** on `{{FINDINGS_FILE}}`; if the additions push it over, retire or promote something *in the same edit*, and report the line count in the wrap-up summary.{{ENDIF}} Do not talk yourself past a failure — each rule exists because that defect shipped once already.

Check for contradictions before finishing: if a new entry disagrees with an existing one, resolve it now rather than leaving both. A findings file that contradicts itself is worse than none, because it is trusted.

If the session surfaced nothing that clears the bar, say so explicitly in the summary rather than skipping silently — "no new findings" is itself information about the session.

{{IF CLAIMS_FILE}}### 7. Reconcile the claims index ({{CLAIMS_FILE}})

Step 6 records what the project now knows. This asks what that lets it **assert**
— and it is the step most likely to be skipped, because a claim register only
ever hurts in the short term.

Walk the session's findings against the table:

1. **Did a measurement move an evidence cell?** `○ asserted` → `●● measured`
   needs the commit in the cell. Nothing else moves it.
2. **Did a search move a novelty cell?** Only `researchflow` earns that, and only
   with a citation to what was searched.
3. **Did anything move a row DOWN?** A number that came in worse, a paper that
   turned out to anticipate us, a caveat that narrows the population. **Report
   these first.** A register that only ratchets upward is not being maintained,
   it is being decorated.
4. **Did the session surface evidence AGAINST a claim?** Put it in the claim's
   own file under *what would sink it*, now, while the reasoning is fresh.

**Never promote on the novelty axis because the evidence axis moved.** Measuring
something more carefully does not make it newer. If a row outgrew its cell, move
the reasoning into `{{CLAIMS_DIR}}/` and leave a pointer.

If nothing moved, say so in the summary. "No claim changed" is information — a
session that measured a lot and moved no claim is worth noticing.

{{ENDIF}}{{IF ARCH_DOC}}
### 8. Update the architecture doc ({{ARCH_DOC}})

Steps 5 and 6 record what happened and what the project knows. This records **what the project now is** — and it is the only one of the three with no other author. `{{ARCH_DOC}}` is read at every `startup` and by the `claim` skill before any design work; nothing else in the workflow writes it. Left to itself it does not stay merely incomplete, it becomes wrong, and it is wrong in the most expensive place: the document a session trusts before it has looked at any code.

Update it when this session changed the project's **shape**, not merely its contents:

- A new module, service, or boundary that other code will now route through
- A dependency direction reversed, added, or cut
- An abstraction replaced, or a second implementation of one that was singular
- A baseline tech choice made or overturned — a datastore, a transport, a framework
- A constraint that is now enforced somewhere different, or newly enforced at all
- An entry point, deployment target, or integration that did not exist before

Leave it alone for work that fits the shape already described: implementing inside an existing boundary, fixing a bug, adding tests, tuning, renaming. **Most sessions should not touch this file.** An architecture doc that absorbs every change stops being a map and becomes a changelog, at which point nobody reads it and the `startup` step that loads it is pure cost.

Two things worth getting right:

1. **Write current state, not history.** "The scheduler talks to the queue over gRPC" — not "we switched the scheduler from HTTP to gRPC". The session log in step 5 is where the change belongs; this file describes the world as it stands, so a reader who has never seen the diff is oriented correctly.
2. **Check the folder-level docs too.** `{{ARCH_DOC}}` files in subdirectories carry local conventions and are loaded on demand when work touches those folders. A session that changed one folder's conventions and updated only the root doc has left the more specific — and more trusted — file wrong.

If the session changed shape and you cannot describe the new shape in a sentence or two, that is worth surfacing to the user rather than writing something vague: it usually means the change did something the design did not anticipate.

Commit it with the other close-out edits, and say in the wrap-up summary whether you touched it — including "architecture unchanged", which is the common and correct answer.
{{ENDIF}}
### 8. Notice workflow friction (offer flowtune)

Scan the session for signals that the documented workflow drifted from how you actually worked:

- A phase was skipped without it being a deliberate "this phase doesn't apply" decision
- You ran a step (e.g., `gstack:simplify`) that isn't in the documented chain — repeatedly
- A "Recommended" skill choice was overridden the same way 2+ times
- Someone said "we should have a skill for X" or built something custom that filled an obvious gap
- The verify chain converged on a different shape than the documented default

If 1+ signal appears, offer to run `wovenflow:flowtune` via `AskUserQuestion`:

- **Run flowtune now** — propose specific edits to CLAUDE.md's Standard workstream
- **Defer** — note the signals in the checkpoint (step 5) for next session
- **Skip** — this drift is intentional / one-off

If no signals, skip this step silently — the workflow is working.

### 9. Reserve the next task

Identify the most natural follow-on, in priority order:

1. A task that depends on what just shipped (now unblocked)
2. The next-highest-priority `{{IF TASK_SOURCE_GITHUB}}{{LABEL_READY}}{{ENDIF}}{{IF TASK_SOURCE_TASKS}}{{STATUS_READY}}{{ENDIF}}` task
3. A previously-blocked task that may now be unblocked by other work

Confirm the choice with the user using `AskUserQuestion`. On confirmation, **only flip the status** — do not run the full claim flow. The session-start steps ({{IF ARCH_DOC}}architecture refresh, {{ENDIF}}clarifying questions{{IF TASK_SOURCE_GITHUB}}, plan comment{{ENDIF}}) belong at the start of the next session, when the user is paying fresh attention.

{{IF TASK_SOURCE_GITHUB}}
```
gh issue edit <id> {{IF AGENT_LABEL}}--add-label {{AGENT_LABEL}} {{ENDIF}}--add-label {{LABEL_IN_PROGRESS}} --remove-label {{LABEL_READY}} --add-assignee @me
```
{{ENDIF}}{{IF TASK_SOURCE_TASKS}}
```
{{RECORDS_HELPER}} tasks set <id> --status {{STATUS_IN_PROGRESS}} --owner @me
```

If the helper reports a change, commit it:

```
git add {{TASKS_FILE}}
git commit -m "chore(tasks): reserve <id>"
```
{{ENDIF}}

**Why reserve, not claim:** starting next session with a task already `{{IF TASK_SOURCE_GITHUB}}{{LABEL_IN_PROGRESS}}{{ENDIF}}{{IF TASK_SOURCE_TASKS}}{{STATUS_IN_PROGRESS}}{{ENDIF}}` is simpler than starting cold — `startup` resumes a known target instead of picking from scratch. But a stale architecture refresh and a plan comment posted at session-end aren't useful; they belong at session-start. So wrap-up reserves only; the next session's `startup` runs the project's `claim` skill (which is idempotent on the status flip), completing the architecture refresh, clarifying questions, and plan-comment steps with fresh attention.

If no clear next task exists, skip this step. Next session's `startup` will pick from cold.

## Anti-patterns

- **Leaving commits unpushed** — work is invisible to others; risk of loss on disk failure
- **Leaving dead code and data behind** — orphaned modules, superseded approaches, failed-run output dirs, scratch fixtures. They rot in place: future readers can't tell what's load-bearing, and the repo inflates run after run. Remove (git is the archive for code) or archive with a one-line why
- **Stale status** — the task tracker diverges from reality; other contributors trust stale data
- **No checkpoint** — future-you re-derives context from scratch, costing 10-30 minutes per session
- **Auto-reserving without confirmation** — wrap-up should *propose* the next task and confirm with the user before flipping status; never silently take over a new task at session-end
- **Running the full claim flow at wrap-up** — architecture refresh, clarifying questions, and plan comments belong at session-start when the user is paying fresh attention. Reserve flips status only; the next `startup` completes the claim.
